\section{BEP Reference}
\label{sec:bep-reference}

The BitTorrent protocol is specified through a series of BitTorrent Enhancement
Proposals (BEPs). This appendix provides a reference to the BEPs most relevant
to this implementation, along with brief descriptions of their content.

\subsection{Core Protocol BEPs}

\begin{description}

\item[BEP 3: The BitTorrent Protocol Specification]
The foundational specification defining the core protocol. Covers the metainfo
file format (bencoding, info dictionary structure), tracker HTTP protocol
(announce requests and responses), and peer wire protocol (handshake, messages,
piece exchange). This document implements BEP 3 as its primary reference.

\item[BEP 20: Peer ID Conventions]
Defines the standard format for peer IDs, which are 20-byte identifiers sent
in tracker announces and peer handshakes. The Azureus-style format uses
\texttt{-XX0000-} where \texttt{XX} is a two-character client ID and
\texttt{0000} is a version number, followed by random bytes. Shadow-style
uses a single character followed by version numbers. Our client uses an
Azureus-style peer ID with the prefix \texttt{-LB0001-} (Literate BitTorrent,
version 0.0.0.1).

\item[BEP 23: Tracker Returns Compact Peer Lists]
Specifies the compact representation of peer lists in tracker responses.
Instead of a list of dictionaries with \texttt{peer id}, \texttt{ip}, and
\texttt{port} keys, peers are encoded as a concatenated byte string with
6 bytes per peer: 4 bytes for the IPv4 address and 2 bytes for the port
(both in network byte order). This reduces response size significantly.
Our implementation supports both compact and dictionary formats.

\item[BEP 10: Extension Protocol]
Defines a mechanism for extending the peer protocol without breaking
backwards compatibility. Extensions are negotiated in the handshake through
the reserved bytes (specifically bit 20 of the reserved field). The extension
handshake message (id 20) allows peers to exchange supported extensions.
Not implemented in this client, but required for many modern extensions.

\end{description}

\subsection{Tracker Protocol BEPs}

\begin{description}

\item[BEP 7: IPv6 Tracker Extension]
Extends BEP 3 to support IPv6 peer addresses. Trackers may return an
\texttt{peers6} key containing compact IPv6 peer addresses (18 bytes each:
16 for the address, 2 for the port). Not implemented in this client.

\item[BEP 15: UDP Tracker Protocol]
Defines a binary UDP-based tracker protocol as an alternative to the
HTTP-based protocol in BEP 3. UDP trackers have lower overhead and can
handle more simultaneous requests. The protocol uses a connect-announce
model with 64-bit connection IDs for security. Not implemented in this
client, which uses HTTP trackers only.

\item[Scrape Protocol (Unofficial Specification)]
The scrape extension (not formally a BEP) allows clients to query aggregate
statistics about torrents (number of seeders, leechers, and completed
downloads) without affecting announce state. Documented in the unofficial
BitTorrent specification. Not implemented in this client.

\end{description}

\subsection{Peer Protocol Extensions}

\begin{description}

\item[BEP 5: DHT Protocol]
Specifies a distributed hash table for decentralized peer discovery,
eliminating dependence on centralized trackers. Peers store and retrieve
contact information using the Kademlia algorithm. The DHT is bootstrapped
through well-known nodes or peers that advertise DHT support in the handshake.
See Section~\ref{sec:future-dht} for discussion.

\item[BEP 6: Fast Extension]
Adds messages for faster piece exchange: \texttt{Have All}, \texttt{Have None}
(replacing large bitfields), \texttt{Suggest Piece}, \texttt{Reject Request},
and \texttt{Allowed Fast}. These optimize initial connection setup and
improve behavior under choking. Negotiated through bit 2 of the reserved
bytes. Not implemented in this client.

\item[BEP 9: Extension for Peers to Send Metadata Files]
Enables peers to exchange the metainfo (info dictionary) directly, supporting
magnet links that contain only the info hash. Peers request metadata pieces
and verify the assembled result against the info hash. Requires BEP 10.
See Section~\ref{sec:future-magnet} for discussion.

\item[BEP 11: Peer Exchange (PEX)]
Allows peers to share information about other peers they know, reducing
tracker load and improving swarm connectivity. PEX messages contain added
and dropped peer lists, with flags indicating peer capabilities. Requires
BEP 10. See Section~\ref{sec:future-pex} for discussion.

\item[BEP 29: uTorrent Transport Protocol (uTP)]
Specifies a reliable transport protocol over UDP with built-in congestion
control. uTP achieves better network friendliness than TCP by using
LEDBAT (Low Extra Delay Background Transport), which backs off when latency
increases. See Section~\ref{sec:future-utp} for discussion.

\end{description}

\subsection{Metainfo Extensions}

\begin{description}

\item[BEP 12: Multitracker Metadata Extension]
Adds an \texttt{announce-list} key to the metainfo file, containing tiers
of tracker URLs. Clients try trackers in each tier randomly, falling back
to the next tier on failure. This provides redundancy and load distribution.
Partially implemented in this client (announce-list is parsed but only the
primary tracker is used).

\item[BEP 17: HTTP Seeding (Hoffman-style)]
Enables HTTP/HTTPS servers to act as sources for piece data, useful when
a web server hosts the same content that is being distributed via BitTorrent.
The \texttt{httpseeds} key in the metainfo lists seed URLs. Not implemented.

\item[BEP 19: WebSeed - HTTP/FTP Seeding (GetRight-style)]
Similar to BEP 17 but with a different URL scheme (\texttt{url-list}) and
different byte-range semantics. Allows HTTP and FTP servers to supplement
the BitTorrent swarm. Not implemented in this client.

\item[BEP 27: Private Torrents]
Defines the \texttt{private} flag in the info dictionary. When set to 1,
clients must not use DHT or PEX, obtaining peers only from the tracker.
This supports private trackers that require authentication. Our client
respects the private flag (by not implementing DHT or PEX at all).

\item[BEP 47: Padding Files and Extended File Attributes]
Enables piece-aligned files within a torrent, improving piece boundary
alignment for multi-file torrents. Also adds support for symbolic links
and extended file attributes. Useful for efficient piece storage but not
implemented in this client.

\end{description}


\section{Test Vectors}
\label{sec:test-vectors}

This appendix provides test vectors for validating bencode implementations
and info hash computations. Each example shows the raw bencoded bytes,
their hexadecimal representation, and the decoded value.

\subsection{Bencode Examples}

\subsubsection{Integers}

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lll}
\toprule
\textbf{Bencoded} & \textbf{Hex} & \textbf{Value} \\
\midrule
\texttt{i0e} & \texttt{69 30 65} & 0 \\
\texttt{i42e} & \texttt{69 34 32 65} & 42 \\
\texttt{i-3e} & \texttt{69 2d 33 65} & $-3$ \\
\texttt{i1234567890e} & \texttt{69 31 32 33 34 35 36 37 38 39 30 65} & 1234567890 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\noindent Invalid integer encodings (should be rejected):
\begin{itemize}
\item \texttt{i-0e} --- negative zero is forbidden
\item \texttt{i03e} --- leading zeros are forbidden
\item \texttt{i+5e} --- explicit positive sign is forbidden
\item \texttt{ie} --- empty integer is invalid
\end{itemize}

\subsubsection{Byte Strings}

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lll}
\toprule
\textbf{Bencoded} & \textbf{Hex} & \textbf{Value} \\
\midrule
\texttt{0:} & \texttt{30 3a} & (empty string) \\
\texttt{4:spam} & \texttt{34 3a 73 70 61 6d} & ``spam'' \\
\texttt{3:cow} & \texttt{33 3a 63 6f 77} & ``cow'' \\
\texttt{12:Hello World!} & \texttt{31 32 3a 48 65 6c 6c 6f 20 57 6f 72 6c 64 21} & ``Hello World!'' \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\noindent Binary strings (containing non-printable bytes):

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{ll}
\toprule
\textbf{Bencoded (hex)} & \textbf{Value} \\
\midrule
\texttt{34 3a 00 01 02 03} & 4 bytes: 0x00, 0x01, 0x02, 0x03 \\
\texttt{32 30 3a} + 20 SHA1 bytes & 20-byte hash (typical piece hash) \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\subsubsection{Lists}

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{ll}
\toprule
\textbf{Bencoded} & \textbf{Value} \\
\midrule
\texttt{le} & Empty list: \texttt{[]} \\
\texttt{l4:spami42ee} & \texttt{["spam", 42]} \\
\texttt{li1ei2ei3ee} & \texttt{[1, 2, 3]} \\
\texttt{l4:spam4:eggse} & \texttt{["spam", "eggs"]} \\
\texttt{ll4:spamee} & Nested: \texttt{[["spam"]]} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\subsubsection{Dictionaries}

Dictionaries require keys in sorted lexicographic order by raw bytes:

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{ll}
\toprule
\textbf{Bencoded} & \textbf{Value} \\
\midrule
\texttt{de} & Empty dict: \texttt{\{\}} \\
\texttt{d3:cow3:moo4:spam4:eggse} & \texttt{\{"cow": "moo", "spam": "eggs"\}} \\
\texttt{d4:spaml1:a1:bee} & \texttt{\{"spam": ["a", "b"]\}} \\
\texttt{d9:publisher3:bob17:publisher-webpage15:www.example.come} & Longer keys example \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\noindent Key ordering example. The following dictionary:
\begin{verbatim}
{"a": 1, "b": 2, "aa": 3}
\end{verbatim}
\noindent Must be encoded with keys in byte order: \texttt{a} (0x61), \texttt{aa} (0x61 0x61), \texttt{b} (0x62):
\begin{verbatim}
d1:ai1e2:aai3e1:bi2ee
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Info Hash Examples}
\label{sec:info-hash-examples}

The info hash is computed by taking the SHA1 hash of the bencoded \texttt{info}
dictionary. The following examples use well-known torrents whose info hashes
have been verified across multiple clients.

\subsubsection{Example: Minimal Single-File Torrent}

Consider a minimal info dictionary for a single file:

\begin{verbatim}
{
  "length": 65536,
  "name": "test.txt",
  "piece length": 16384,
  "pieces": <80 bytes: 4 SHA1 hashes>
}
\end{verbatim}

\noindent Bencoded (hex representation, with \texttt{<pieces>} as placeholder):
\begin{verbatim}
d6:lengthi65536e4:name8:test.txt12:piece lengthi16384e
6:pieces80:<80 bytes>e
\end{verbatim}

\noindent The info hash is \texttt{SHA1(bencoded\_info\_dict)}.

\subsubsection{Example: Known Public Torrent}

The ``Big Buck Bunny'' video (480p version from archive.org) is a commonly
used test torrent. Its info hash is:

\begin{verbatim}
dd8255ecdc7ca55fb0bbf81323d87062db1f6d1c
\end{verbatim}

\noindent For tracker announces, this hash is URL-encoded. Each byte becomes
\texttt{\%XX} except for unreserved characters (alphanumerics, hyphen, period,
underscore, tilde). Our \texttt{url\_encode} function in the tracker module
handles this conversion.

\subsubsection{Example: Ubuntu ISO}

Ubuntu Linux ISOs are distributed via BitTorrent. The Ubuntu 22.04 LTS
desktop ISO (ubuntu-22.04-desktop-amd64.iso) has info hash:

\begin{verbatim}
3b245504cf5f11bbdbe1201cea6a6bf45aee1bc0
\end{verbatim}

\subsubsection{Computing Info Hashes}

To verify your implementation:

\begin{enumerate}
\item Download a known \texttt{.torrent} file
\item Parse it and extract the \texttt{info} dictionary
\item Re-encode the info dictionary to canonical bencode
\item Compute SHA1 of the bencoded bytes
\item Compare with the expected hash
\end{enumerate}

\noindent Common mistakes that produce incorrect info hashes:
\begin{itemize}
\item Not sorting dictionary keys lexicographically by raw bytes
\item Modifying the info dictionary after parsing (changing piece boundaries)
\item Encoding integers with leading zeros
\item Using UTF-8 string comparison instead of byte comparison for key ordering
\end{itemize}


\section{Future Work}
\label{sec:future-work}

This implementation covers the core BitTorrent protocol as specified in BEP 3,
sufficient for downloading torrents from HTTP trackers. However, modern
BitTorrent clients implement numerous extensions that improve performance,
reliability, and functionality. This section discusses the most important
extensions not implemented in this client.

\subsection{Distributed Hash Table (DHT)}
\label{sec:future-dht}

The Distributed Hash Table (BEP 5) enables decentralized peer discovery,
eliminating dependence on centralized trackers. This is perhaps the most
significant extension to the BitTorrent ecosystem.

\paragraph{How DHT Works.}
DHT uses the Kademlia algorithm, where each node has a 160-bit identifier
(typically derived from the peer's key pair). Nodes maintain routing tables
organized by XOR distance from their own ID. The info hash serves as the
key for looking up peers sharing a particular torrent.

To find peers for a torrent:
\begin{enumerate}
\item Compute the XOR distance between the info hash and known nodes
\item Query the closest nodes for even closer nodes
\item Iterate until finding nodes that have announced the torrent
\item Retrieve the list of peers from those nodes
\end{enumerate}

\paragraph{Implementation Considerations.}
Adding DHT to this client would require:
\begin{itemize}
\item A UDP socket for DHT messages (separate from TCP peer connections)
\item A Kademlia routing table with bucket refresh logic
\item KRPC message encoding (bencode over UDP)
\item Bootstrap logic to join the DHT network
\item Announce and lookup operations
\item Respecting the \texttt{private} flag (no DHT for private torrents)
\end{itemize}

\paragraph{Benefits.}
DHT provides resilience against tracker failures and enables magnet links
(which contain only the info hash). Most public torrents can be downloaded
using DHT alone, without any tracker.

\subsection{Micro Transport Protocol (uTP)}
\label{sec:future-utp}

uTP (BEP 29) is a reliable transport protocol over UDP that provides
congestion control designed to minimize impact on other network traffic.

\paragraph{Why uTP Matters.}
TCP's congestion control can cause latency spikes for other applications
(gaming, VoIP) when BitTorrent saturates the connection. uTP uses LEDBAT
(Low Extra Delay Background Transport), which monitors one-way delay and
backs off when latency increases. This makes BitTorrent traffic less
disruptive to other applications sharing the same connection.

\paragraph{Implementation Considerations.}
Adding uTP would require:
\begin{itemize}
\item A UDP socket multiplexing multiple virtual connections
\item Reliable delivery with selective acknowledgments
\item LEDBAT congestion control with delay measurements
\item Connection state machines for each virtual connection
\item Integration with the existing peer protocol (same messages, different transport)
\end{itemize}

\paragraph{Benefits.}
uTP enables connections through some NAT configurations that block TCP,
and provides network-friendly congestion control. Most modern clients
prefer uTP over TCP when both are available.

\subsection{Peer Exchange (PEX)}
\label{sec:future-pex}

Peer Exchange (BEP 11) allows connected peers to share information about
other peers they know, supplementing tracker and DHT discovery.

\paragraph{How PEX Works.}
Peers periodically send PEX messages containing:
\begin{itemize}
\item \texttt{added}: compact list of new peers discovered since last PEX
\item \texttt{added.f}: flags for each added peer (supports encryption, seeder, etc.)
\item \texttt{dropped}: compact list of peers no longer connected
\end{itemize}

PEX is negotiated through the extension protocol (BEP 10) during handshake.

\paragraph{Implementation Considerations.}
Adding PEX would require:
\begin{itemize}
\item BEP 10 extension protocol support in the handshake
\item Tracking peer connectivity changes
\item Rate limiting PEX messages (typically every 60 seconds)
\item Respecting the \texttt{private} flag (no PEX for private torrents)
\end{itemize}

\paragraph{Benefits.}
PEX provides rapid peer discovery once connected to the swarm, often faster
than tracker announces. It also reduces tracker load.

\subsection{Magnet Links}
\label{sec:future-magnet}

Magnet links (BEP 9) allow downloading torrents using only the info hash,
without a separate \texttt{.torrent} file.

\paragraph{Magnet Link Format.}
A typical magnet link looks like:
\begin{verbatim}
magnet:?xt=urn:btih:<info-hash>&dn=<display-name>&tr=<tracker-url>
\end{verbatim}

The \texttt{xt} (exact topic) parameter contains the info hash. Optional
parameters include display name (\texttt{dn}) and tracker URLs (\texttt{tr}).

\paragraph{How Magnet Downloads Work.}
\begin{enumerate}
\item Parse the info hash from the magnet link
\item Find peers via DHT or provided tracker
\item Request metadata from peers using the extension protocol (BEP 9)
\item Verify the assembled metadata matches the info hash
\item Proceed with normal piece download
\end{enumerate}

\paragraph{Implementation Considerations.}
Supporting magnet links would require:
\begin{itemize}
\item Magnet URI parser
\item DHT support (for peer discovery without tracker)
\item BEP 10 extension protocol
\item Metadata exchange messages (request, data, reject)
\item Ability to start download with only info hash
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Other Notable Extensions}

Several other extensions merit mention:

\paragraph{BEP 6: Fast Extension.}
Adds \texttt{Have All}, \texttt{Have None}, \texttt{Suggest Piece},
\texttt{Reject Request}, and \texttt{Allowed Fast} messages. These optimize
connection startup (no need to send large bitfields) and improve behavior
under choking (explicitly reject pending requests rather than leaving them
hanging).

\paragraph{BEP 14: Local Service Discovery.}
Allows discovering peers on the local network via multicast, useful for
LAN-only sharing or supplementing Internet swarms with fast local peers.

\paragraph{BEP 40: Canonical Peer Priority.}
Defines a deterministic ordering for peer preference, ensuring that peers
in a swarm converge on similar connection graphs. This improves piece
availability without explicit coordination.

\paragraph{Message Stream Encryption (MSE/PE).}
Obfuscates BitTorrent traffic to avoid detection and throttling by ISPs.
Uses RC4 encryption with keys derived from the info hash. MSE was developed
by Azureus/Vuze but never formally standardized as a BEP.

\subsection{Recommendations for Extension}

For those wishing to extend this client, we suggest the following order:

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{BEP 10 Extension Protocol}: Foundation for most modern extensions
\item \textbf{BEP 6 Fast Extension}: Simple to implement, improves performance
\item \textbf{BEP 11 PEX}: Improves peer discovery with minimal complexity
\item \textbf{BEP 5 DHT}: Significant undertaking, but enables magnet links
\item \textbf{BEP 9 Metadata Exchange}: Enables magnet links once DHT works
\item \textbf{BEP 29 uTP}: Complex but worthwhile for network friendliness
\end{enumerate}

Each extension builds on previous ones, and the core protocol implemented
in this document provides a solid foundation for all of them.


\section{Glossary}
\label{sec:glossary}

\begin{description}

\item[Announce] An HTTP request to the tracker indicating the client's
  presence in the swarm and current download/upload statistics.

\item[Bencode] The encoding format used throughout BitTorrent for torrent
  files and tracker responses. Supports integers, byte strings, lists,
  and dictionaries.

\item[Bitfield] A bitmap indicating which pieces a peer has. Sent immediately
  after the handshake to establish initial piece availability.

\item[Block] A sub-piece unit, typically 16 KB (16384 bytes). Pieces are
  requested and transferred as blocks.

\item[Choke] A state where a peer refuses to upload to us. Peers choke
  connections they are not willing to serve, typically due to low reciprocity.

\item[DHT] Distributed Hash Table. A decentralized peer discovery mechanism
  using the Kademlia algorithm.

\item[Handshake] The initial message exchange when two peers connect,
  verifying they share the same protocol and info hash.

\item[Info Dictionary] The portion of the metainfo file describing the
  content: file names, sizes, piece length, and piece hashes.

\item[Info Hash] The SHA1 hash of the bencoded info dictionary. Uniquely
  identifies a torrent across the network.

\item[Interested] A state indicating that we want pieces from a peer.
  Peers will only upload to interested connections.

\item[Leecher] A peer that is still downloading and has an incomplete copy
  of the content.

\item[Magnet Link] A URI containing an info hash, allowing downloads without
  a separate torrent file.

\item[Metainfo File] A \texttt{.torrent} file containing tracker URLs, the
  info dictionary, and optional metadata.

\item[Peer] Another BitTorrent client connected to the same swarm.

\item[Peer ID] A 20-byte identifier uniquely identifying a client instance.
  Sent in tracker announces and peer handshakes.

\item[Piece] A fixed-size chunk of the content, typically 256 KB to 4 MB.
  Each piece is verified independently using its SHA1 hash.

\item[Scrape] A tracker request returning aggregate statistics about a
  torrent without affecting announce state.

\item[Seeder] A peer that has the complete file and only uploads.

\item[Swarm] All peers sharing a particular torrent, identified by its
  info hash.

\item[Tracker] A server that maintains a list of peers for each torrent
  and responds to announce requests.

\item[Unchoke] The opposite of choke; a state where a peer is willing to
  upload to us.

\item[uTP] Micro Transport Protocol. A reliable UDP-based transport with
  LEDBAT congestion control.

\end{description}


\section{Safety Properties}
\label{sec:safety}

This crate is written entirely in safe Rust. No \texttt{unsafe} blocks appear in
the literate sources (\texttt{nw/*.nw}) or generated code (\texttt{src/*.rs}).
This property can be verified by searching for the \texttt{unsafe} keyword:

\begin{verbatim}
grep -r "unsafe" nw/*.nw src/*.rs
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Rationale}

The PRD for this project specifies ``no unsafe unless justified.'' For a
pedagogical BitTorrent client, safety is more valuable than the marginal
performance gains that unsafe code might provide. Every algorithm in this
implementation uses safe abstractions:

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Bencode parsing}: Uses iterators and slice operations, never raw pointer arithmetic
\item \textbf{Network I/O}: Tokio's safe async abstractions handle all socket operations
\item \textbf{SHA1 hashing}: The \texttt{sha1} crate provides a safe API
\item \textbf{Bit manipulation}: The \texttt{bitvec} crate wraps any unsafe internals
\item \textbf{Byte buffers}: The \texttt{bytes} crate provides safe views into memory
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Dependencies and Unsafe}

While this crate contains no unsafe code, several dependencies necessarily use
unsafe internally for performance or FFI requirements:

\begin{description}
\item[libc] FFI bindings to the C standard library, required for OS interactions
\item[ring] Cryptographic operations for TLS (via rustls), uses carefully audited unsafe
\item[tokio/mio] Async runtime requires unsafe for syscall integration
\item[bytes] High-performance byte buffers use unsafe for zero-copy operations
\end{description}

These dependencies are:
\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Necessary}: Each provides functionality that cannot be implemented
  in safe Rust (FFI, syscalls, hardware crypto instructions)
\item \textbf{Well-audited}: All are widely used crates with extensive testing
  and security review
\item \textbf{Minimal}: We use \texttt{reqwest} with \texttt{rustls-tls} instead
  of OpenSSL to reduce the unsafe surface area
\end{enumerate}

Tools like \texttt{cargo-geiger} can provide detailed unsafe statistics for the
full dependency tree. The choice of rustls over OpenSSL specifically reduces
the amount of C code (and thus potential memory safety issues) in the TLS stack.


